Roxie's on inside our cell is considered  to be a recycle center of the cell now  if we imagine cell to be a big Factory  then the recycling hub is the peroxisome  and there are key aspects of recycling  taking place in the peroxisome most  notably the fatty acid oxidation  especially the long-chain fatty acid and  branch fatty acids D amino acids and  even sometimes of poly amines are  getting oxidized in the peroxisome  and the whole plethora of tasks that  these paroxysm takes place is done by  the enzymes inside the peroxisomes and  peroxisome has a huge variety of oxidase  enzymes  it also has catalase now oxidase can  attach or oxidize a group and create  hydrogen peroxide now catalase our other  set of enzymes which can degrade  hydrogen peroxide because hydrogen  peroxide is harmful for the cell it's a  reactive oxygen species to water and  oxygen so catalase oxidase and many  other like peroxisomal proteins need to  be delivered to the peroxisome paroxysm  don't have their own protein production  machinery like mitochondria so all the  genes that are actually coding for the  peroxisomal enzymes and nuclear genes so  definitely the peroxisomal enzymes are  transcribed in the nucleus and then they  are translated into the ER so  peroxisomal enzymes are transcribed into  the nucleus translated into the ER and  from there they are going to be  delivered to the paroxysm so this video  is all about how this delivery mechanism  works so one question we should first  ask that how peroxisomal enzymes are  selectively delivered to peroxisome not  to any other places and the answer is  quite simple just like we have seen in  case of mitochondria there is a  mitochondrial  localization signal which would drive  the transport of the protein to  mitochondria and not anyplace else  peroxisome distant proteins also have  the peroxisomal translocation signal or  pts most of the cases it has a pts one  signal which is basically serine leucine  and said in lysine and reducing but some  of the cases-- they also have a pts 2  signal which is very uncommon now the  mitochondrial protein transport is a bit  different from peroxisomal protein  transport because in mitochondria the  proteins that need to be transported  need to be bound to chaperones and  maintained in an unfolded state and then  delivered to the mitochondria in  comparison to that peroxisomal proteins  let's say catalase which is an important  proximal protein can be transported to  peroxisome in its native situation even  the cofactors it's attached so the heme  group of the peroxisomal catalase is  that attached while it is transported  now how do you know that this particular  sequence that is driving the protein  into mitochondria or peroxisome is  necessary and sufficient for this  delivery mechanism so let's say you have  a protein which is actually having a  mitochondrial localization signal so  since it has a mitochondrial  localization signal it would normally be  delivered to mitochondria but let's say  you do a thought experiment where you  cleave the signal and put a lysosome at  put a peroxisomal translocation signal  into that then what would happen  scientists have observed in with  experiments that this protein is now  delivered to paroxysm instead of  mitochondria so these signals are super  important more specific in in  experiments have shown that when  scientists use the cytosolic GFP which  is normally present in the cytosol and  it would glow like this under a  fluorescence microscope now when they  take the cytosolic GFP and attach a  proximal translocation signal to it  instead of staying in the cytosol  cytosolic gfp is now transported to the  peroxisome cell now you can see speckles  inside the cells which are like GFP  positive so basically now the peroxisome  cell highlighted in gfp that means the  cytosolic gfp has been now transported  to the peroxisomes so that tells us this  particular C terminal sequence or  peroxisome target sequence is efficient  to transport the paroxysm protein to the  peroxisomes now let's just talk about  this process in a bit more detail so  here is our protein of interest let's  say oxidase enzyme that need to be  transported into the paroxysm and on the  surface of the paroxysm there would be  pics family of translocon now point to  be noted that these paroxysm the protein  that need to be delivered to the  paroxysm should have the proximal target  sequence  now this proximal target sequence can  bind to paroxysm proximal target  sequence receptor or pts are normally it  is also known as X 5 so what would  happen is that these the the overall the  basics of it is like this PT SR would  take it to the pix family translocon and  it would interact with pix family  translocon and allow the protein to be  channeled inside the peroxisome but this  process is bit difficult and let's just  talk about it in a little bit more  detail say so the translocon complex the  pics translocon complex is actually made  up of four components X 2 X 10 which is  a main translocon channel  X 12 and pix 14 so basically the pts are  or the pics 5 points to pix 14 along  with the protein that need to be  translocated inside the inside the  peroxisome now the whole pix 14 and pics  5 complex along with the protein that  need to be translocated mobilize get  mobilized to the translocon channel  complex then the protein with the pts  sequence gets into the pics 10  translocon channel and the  PTS are our picks five gets dissociated  and it can be used for another cycle of  protein from protein targeting to the X  14 and that is how the protein which is  important for peroxisomal normal  physiology gets inside the paroxysm now  you these translocon channels are not  proper there could be several mutations  in the translocon channel it has been  seen that there are multiple genetic  disorders of these translocon channel  give rise to severe problems in terms of  physiology so these protein  translocation into